[metadata]
title: Puter.js: Full Backend For AI Agents, Auth, Storage, Database and 500+ LLMs
channel: @octopusinc_
duration: 09:06
source: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MYFlhIbvOBc
upload_date: 20260819
transcript_source: captions+frames
route: visual

---

[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Building an AI app usually stops at the same place. You write the front end, you get it looking good, and then you need an AI key. That means signing up with OpenAI, adding a card, putting money in before you can send a single request, and then'

[00:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'hiding that key somewhere it won\'t leak. And after all that, every person who uses your app is spending your money. That\'s the part nobody warns you about. If your app gets shared around, your'

[00:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'bill grows with it. Puter.js changes who pays. It\'s an open-source JavaScript library, around 43,000 stars on GitHub. And it gives your front end code access to AI models, cloud storage, a database,'

[00:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'auth, and hosting. No keys, no server, one script tag. The part that matters for your wallet is what they call the user pays model, where each person who uses your app'

[00:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'covers their own usage through their own Puter account. Your infrastructure cost stays at zero, no matter how many users show up. The trick that makes this work with any AI tool is a file Puter publishes at docs.puter.com/llms.txt.'

[01:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'It\'s the whole API reference in one page, written for language models to read. So, instead of hoping ChatGPT remembers a library it may have never'

[01:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'seen, you point it at the file. You can run this prompt to build a simple to-do list app using Puter.js. It work with any coding agent like Claude, Cursor, Code X, Lovable, and more. Just copy the'

[01:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'command and run in your coding agent. Watch what it does. It writes one HTML file. There\'s a script tag pointing to js.puter.com/v2, and then the actual logic. Saving a task'

[01:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is puter.kv.set. Reading tasks back is puter.kv.get. That KV stands for key-value, and it\'s a cloud database you did not create, did not configure, and are not paying for.'

[01:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'There\'s no connection string anywhere in this file. Notice also that the AI never stopped to ask me for credentials. That\'s the whole reason this works in one shot, because there\'s nothing to'

[02:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'configure, so the model has nothing to pause on. And one thing that trips up a lot of people, you cannot just double-click this file and open it in your browser. Puter needs a real web server, even a'

[02:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'local one, for security reasons. Opening it straight from your hard drive will fail, and the errors are not obvious. So, I use the live server extension in VS Code. Install it once, then hit go live at the bottom, and it serves the'

[02:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'page on localhost. App loads, looks fine, but the moment I add a task, a pop-up appears asking me to sign in with Puter. That pop-up is doing a lot of work. My code never handled login, never'

[02:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'stored a session, never wrote a password reset flow. Puter handles auth automatically the first time your code touches a cloud service. If I already had a session, it would skip this entirely. So, I sign in, and the task'

[02:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'saves. I\'ll refresh the page to prove it\'s real, and the task is still there because it\'s living in the cloud, not in local storage. The account I just signed in with is where the money model lives.'

[03:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Puter gives every account a monthly allowance of storage, database calls, and AI usage, and you can see exactly what you\'ve used on the usage page inside your account. That page tracks it per resource, so a'

[03:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'user always knows where they stand. When someone needs more than the allowance, they go to the billing page in their own account and pick a plan. The free tier includes 25 megabytes of storage and limited AI and cloud usage. Paid tiers'

[03:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'start at $10 a month for 100 gigabytes, then $25 for 500 gigabytes, and $100 for 2 terabytes, each one raising the AI and cloud limits, too.'

[03:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Now, let\'s build a chat app, which is where the AI side gets interesting. Same approach, I ask for a chat interface using Pooter.js with a drop-down to switch models, and again, I give it the LLMs link. The generated code uses one'

[04:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'function, Pooter.ai.chat. You pass your message, then an options object with a model name. Switching from OpenAI to Claude to Gemini is changing one string, nothing else in the file'

[04:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'changes. Running it, picking a model, sending a message, response comes back streaming. Under the hood, Pooter is routing this through a gateway that covers 500-plus models across a lot of providers, including OpenAI, Anthropic,'

[04:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Google, xAI, Deep Seek, Mistral, and Quinn. Default model is a small, fast one, but you can name whatever you want. For a business, the practical value'

[04:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'there is no vendor lock-in. Model prices move constantly, and switching providers normally means rewriting your integration layer. Same API handles more than text. Vision works by passing an image URL alongside'

[04:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'your prompt, so the model describes what it sees. Image generation is Pooter.ai.text to image. Text-to-speech, speech-to-text, OCR for pulling text out'

[05:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of scanned documents, and short video generation all sit on the same object with the same naming pattern. And there\'s a test mode flag you can pass while you\'re building, so you can hammer'

[05:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the API during development without spending credits. Worth knowing before you leave a loop running by accident. Small API surface is also why token usage drops. Pooter claims up to 90% fewer AI tokens,'

[05:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and up to 12 times less code compared to wiring up something like Supabase, Firebase, or AWS for the same to-do app. Those are their own numbers with a results may vary note, so take them as'

[05:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'directional, but the reason behind it is real. There\'s no config file, no SDK setup, no server code, no deploy step. The model writes less because there\'s less to'

[05:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'write, and it makes fewer mistakes because the syntax is consistent. Security is the part I\'d want an engineering lead to hear. There is no API key anywhere in this project. Not in the front end, not in a .env, not on a'

[06:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'server. Nothing to leak, rotate, or accidentally commit to a public repo. Every request is authenticated as the signed-in user and scoped to just that user.'

[06:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Your app also gets sandboxed automatically, meaning it can only touch its own folder and its own database space inside each person\'s account. It cannot read their other files or another app\'s data.'

[06:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'That sandboxing has a real limitation you should know before you plan a project. Because each user\'s data sits in their own account, one user cannot see another user\'s data. So, a shared leaderboard, a public feed, or a'

[06:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'multiplayer game will not work the obvious way. The documented fix is a serverless worker, which is Pyrs version of back-end endpoints. You write Express-style routes, deploy it, and it runs on your account instead of the'

[06:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'user\'s, giving everyone one shared back end. Just something to plan around rather than discover halfway in. There\'s a whole cloud desktop side to this, too. Going to pyrs.com gives you a full operating system in the browser'

[07:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'with a file manager, a code editor, a terminal, and apps. Files you write from your code show up here in a folder named after your app. So, the storage isn\'t abstract. You can open the folder and see the files. Right clicking a folder'

[07:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'gives you publish as website, which puts a static site live on a free pyrs.site subdomain in a few seconds. There\'s also a command-line tool if you\'d rather deploy from the terminal and a GitHub'

[07:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'action for deploying on every push. For people using agents rather than chat windows, Pyuter runs a hosted MCP server at mcp.pyuter.com. In Claude code, you add it with one'

[07:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'command, authenticate once, and then your agent can list your files, deploy workers, and publish sites on your behalf. That\'s separate from the llms.txt approach. One teaches the agent the'

[08:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'syntax. The other lets the agent actually operate your account. Honest limits, since this isn\'t a sales pitch. Your users need a Pyuter account, which is friction that a normal API key'

[08:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'setup hides from them. You\'re depending on Pyuter staying up and keeping this pricing model, though the core platform being open source on GitHub gives you an exit. Heavy background jobs and scheduled tasks aren\'t the natural fit'

[08:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'here. And if you work somewhere with strict compliance requirements around where data physically lives, that needs checking before you commit. What I\'d actually use it for is prototypes, internal tools, and side'

[08:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'projects where the API bill is the thing stopping you from shipping. Getting a working full stack app from one prompt with real auth, real storage, and real AI, with no key and no server,'

[08:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'removes most of the reason projects die in a folder. Links to the docs and the llms file are below if you want to try the same prompt yourself.'
